    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Rentals</span>
    <h1>Hanoi Motorbike Rental: City Commuters Pre-Ride Inspection</h1>
    <p>A commuter rides the same machine through the same streets so often that its faults start to feel like personality. That comfort is exactly why a pre-ride inspection exists: it replaces the feeling that everything is fine with the observation that everything is fine, in about two minutes, before the day has a chance to turn a small fault into a bad morning. On a rental machine the stakes are higher still, because the commuter did not choose the machine's history and will not own its consequences alone.</p>
    <p>This page is the inspection chapter of the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> cluster for city commuters. Verified rental prices and periods are on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">cost hub</a>, general rental questions are on the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> page, and the machine itself is chosen using the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/city-commuters-bike-choice/">bike-choice page for city commuters</a> before any inspection can matter.</p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#sec-1">The commuter's two-minute habit</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-2">Tires: pressure, tread and the daily pothole check</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-3">Brakes: lever, pedal and the roll test</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-4">Controls, cables and the mirrors</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-5">Lights, horn and being visible</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-6">Visible condition: leaks, loose parts, the stand</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-7">What to do when the inspection finds something</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="sec-1">The commuter's two-minute habit</h2>
    <p>The inspection that gets done is the short one, so this page teaches a fixed two-minute loop, the same every day, walked around the machine in the same order. Fixed order is the trick: a checklist performed identically each morning stops requiring attention and starts running on its own, which means it keeps working on the mornings when the commuter is running late and thinking about anything else. Walk the loop at the bike, not from the doorway.</p>
    <p>On a rental there is a second layer: the comparison against day one. The commuter should photograph the machine at pickup, all four sides, and note anything already scratched or loose, so that every later check measures change rather than guessing at it. The paperwork side of that first day, what gets written down and what the rental records, is part of the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/how-to-rent-a-motorbike-in-hanoi/">how-to-rent guide</a>, and the deposit conversation it feeds is on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/motorbike-rental-deposit-hanoi/">deposit page</a>.</p>
    <p>Two details make the habit stick. First, anchor it to something already daily: the inspection happens while the helmet is still off and the bag is still on the seat, before the phone leaves the pocket. Second, make the loop physically walkable: start at the front wheel, move clockwise around the machine, so the sequence is stored in the feet as much as in the head. A commuter who has to remember a list will skip it; a commuter whose feet know the loop never does.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-2">Tires: pressure, tread and the daily pothole check</h2>
    <p>Tires are the only parts of a commuter's machine that touch the city, and Hanoi's streets test them daily with potholes, kerbs, gravel and the polished paint of junctions. The morning check is a squeeze and a look: press each tire hard to feel that it is not soft, look for cuts, bulges or glass in the tread, and check the wear indicators where the tread blocks meet the smooth strip that says the tire is done. A soft tire is not a schedule problem; it is a cornering problem waiting for the first wet junction.</p>
    <p>For commuters the tire check has one daily addition: the walk to the machine is the moment to look at the ground under it. A flat discovered in the parking lot costs a phone call; a flat discovered two bridges into the commute costs the morning. And after any hard pothole strike during the ride, a ten-second glance at both tires at the next light is the cheapest insurance in this entire page.</p>

    
    <p>Wet season adds one tire question to the loop: is the tread still shedding water? A worn tire on a dry street is a comfort issue; the same tire on the painted arrow of a wet junction is a traction issue, and the difference arrives without warning. When the tread is close to the wear strip, the morning squeeze becomes a morning decision.</p><h2 id="sec-3">Brakes: lever, pedal and the roll test</h2>
    <p>Brakes are checked by feel, and the commuter's advantage is that they know exactly how their machine's brakes felt yesterday. Squeeze the front lever: firm at the same point as usual, no spongy travel, no fade all the way to the grip. Press the rear brake: same bite as yesterday. Then the roll test, before the first junction: push off, roll a couple of metres, and stop with each brake alone, front and rear, feeling for grabbiness, noise or a lever that pumps up differently.</p>
    <p>Any change from yesterday is the signal, not a threshold. A lever that suddenly feels softer, a brake that starts to sing, or a pull to one side under braking all deserve attention before the commute, not after it. The rider-facing questions of what a rental covers when the machine needs work are the counter's to answer, and the questions to ask up front are collected on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/city-commuters-bike-choice/">commuter bike-choice page</a> and the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-4">Controls, cables and the mirrors</h2>
    <p>The controls check is a thirty-second sweep: throttle snaps closed from every position, both brake levers pivot freely and return, the horn and the switches respond, and nothing on the handlebars is loose or rattling. Cables get a glance where they curve at the bars and the steering head, looking for frayed strands or damaged housings, because cables fail at bends and a commuter flexes those bends hundreds of times a week.</p>
    <p>Mirrors matter more on a commute than anywhere else, because the commuter's survival strategy in flow is built on knowing what approaches from behind. Both mirrors clean, both tight, both aimed where they were aimed yesterday. A mirror that vibrates loose every week is not a nuisance, it is the machine telling the commuter something, and it belongs in the morning report to the rental shop rather than a shrug.</p>

    
    <p>A note on standing water and the lights check: after riding through a deep puddle, the brake light and the horn deserve a re-test at the next stop, because water finds electrical joints. It costs five seconds and catches the rare but memorable fault.</p><h2 id="sec-5">Lights, horn and being visible</h2>
    <p>The lights check needs a second person or a wall: with the machine on, confirm headlight, taillight and brake light, front and rear, and check that the lenses are not cracked or packed with dust. Half the commuting year is ridden in the dark in at least one direction, and a brake light that fails quietly is the fault other road users discover first, at the worst moment.</p>
    <p>The horn is the commuter's voice in traffic, and it should sound like it sounded on day one, neither silent nor strangling. Test it in the same quiet spot each morning. Visibility has a reflective side too: the commuter's own jacket and the machine's reflectors do a share of the work, and keeping the taillight lens clean is a two-second task with an all-day payoff. The habits that use all of this in moving traffic are on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/riders-with-a-passenger-traffic-preparation/">traffic preparation page</a> of this cluster, written for pairs but true for everyone.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-6">Visible condition: leaks, loose parts, the stand</h2>
    <p>The condition walk is a slow lap of the machine. Look under it for fresh drops, oil or fuel, which is why the check happens at the same parking spot each morning. Glance at the chain or belt case, the exhaust, the plastics and the fasteners that hold them, and give the side stand the push test: a stand that bends, sticks or sinks into soft ground is how parked bikes fall over in the afternoon. Shake the handlebars gently with the front brake held: nothing should clunk.</p>
    <p>The lap is also when yesterday's damage gets noticed: the new scratch on the leg shield, the bent lever, the missing mirror bolt. On a rental, noticing is the commuter's protection, because faults found and reported by the rider read very differently at the counter from faults discovered by the shop after the return. That difference, and the paperwork that records it, is why the pickup photographs from day one belong in the phone and not in memory.</p>

    
    <p>One habit completes this section: after any ride where something felt different, a grabby brake, a new vibration, a tire that felt vague on a corner, write it down in the phone at the parking spot. The commuter's log is what turns a vague impression into an inspection finding the next morning.</p><h2 id="sec-7">What to do when the inspection finds something</h2>
    <p>The rule is simple: the commute can wait, the fault cannot. Anything in the safety list, tires, brakes, steering, lights, ends the ride before it starts, and the call to the rental shop replaces the morning's first crossing. Anything cosmetic or slow, a rattle, a scuff, a loose mirror, gets reported the same day, in writing, with a photo, so the paper trail matches the physical trail.</p>
    <p>Keep the report factual: what was found, when, and what the commuter did about it. This page invents no shop policies, no turnaround promises, no replacement guarantees, because the terms are the rental agreement's to state and the commuter's to read; the inspection chapter's job ends at noticing and reporting, done properly, every single day of the rental.</p>

    <section class="faq">
      <h2>FAQ</h2>
      <article class="faq-entry">
        <h3>How long should a pre-ride inspection take for a commuter?</h3>
        <p>About two minutes, done as a fixed loop in the same order every morning. The fixed order is what keeps the habit working on the mornings when nobody feels like checking anything.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="faq-entry">
        <h3>What are the most important checks on a rental commuter bike?</h3>
        <p>Tires, brakes and lights, in that order of consequence. Controls, mirrors and visible condition complete the loop, and anything that changed since yesterday deserves attention even if it still works.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="faq-entry">
        <h3>What if I find a fault before the morning commute?</h3>
        <p>Do not ride on a safety fault. Contact the rental shop, report it in writing with a photo, and let the agreement's terms decide the next step. This page does not invent shop policies.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="faq-entry">
        <h3>Why photograph the bike at pickup?</h3>
        <p>So that every later check measures change rather than memory. Faults the rider notices and reports read very differently at the counter from faults the shop discovers after the return.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="faq-entry">
        <h3>Does the inspection change for a daily commute versus occasional riding?</h3>
        <p>Yes. A commuter's inspection leans on comparison, how the machine felt yesterday, because hundreds of repetitions make even small changes meaningful. Occasional riders should lean more on the absolute checks.</p>
      </article>
    </section>
